WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
<v Mike>WWF WrestleMania Challenge!

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.420
<v Mike>Go head to head with eight of the biggest WWF superstars!

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.880
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia!

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.760
<v Mike>A chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.200
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.240
<v Mike>Oh, Sean!

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.680
<v Mike>Sean, this is your, uh, what do they call that?

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.120
<v Mike>Wrestle-sona?

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:43.640
<v Mike>No, no. Your R-sona?

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.340
<v Sean>No, don't do that.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.520
<v Sean>Your ring name.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:47.760
<v Mike>I was in it.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.920
<v Sean>I know. It really took me out of it.

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:51.140
<v Mike>What would be your ring name?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.900
<v Sean>Uh, you know, uh, Shiesty Sean.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.200
<v Mike>Shiesty Sean.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:00:59.180
<v Mike>Yeah, I'm Mikey Murder.

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:00.280
<v Sean>You're Mikey Murder.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:06.140
<v Sean>Yeah. He goes in there, he's murdering those guys, and it's like, the kids don't know that it's

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.720
<v Mike>not real, and they're like, trying to protect their favorite wrestler.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.880
<v Sean>Like, he's gonna murder them!

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.980
<v Sean>Dude, do people, uh, do the people already know that your, uh, childhood screen name was

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.280
<v Mike>Mikey Murder? Yeah, that childhood.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.600
<v Mike>Um, childhood, actually, that's funny, because that's like a whole, that's a whole other conversation

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.400
<v Mike>for everybody, right?

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.700
<v Mike>There, nobody ever had a good AOL screen name.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.840
<v Mike>No, no, no. Nobody's proud of their AOL screen name.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.560
<v Mike>Uh-huh. Mine was Game Player Me, because M-E are my initials.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.660
<v Mike>Uh-huh. So, it was like, I guess some Yoda-ism, where like, you know, me a game player.

00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.800
<v Mike>Like, I, I, I play the games.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.840
<v Mike>Uh, uh, what was yours?

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.580
<v Sean>Oh, I had BBQ 90.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.440
<v Sean>BBQ? Did you love BBQ that much?

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.820
<v Sean>I thought it'd be funny, and then later I had one that's way too cringe-inducing to even

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.680
<v Sean>repeat on the show, so I'm not going to.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:00.620
<v Mike>Is the, is the, is the BBQ one, like, when people were saying like, oh my god, what the

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.140
<v Mike>fuck, BBQ sauce, do you remember?

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:05.700
<v Mike>Like, that would just be like a string of letters that you could throw together?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.440
<v Sean>It definitely preceded that little, so random era of, you know, humor, yeah.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.480
<v Mike>Ahead of the curve, actually.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.840
<v Sean>You should have invested in BBQ.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.160
<v Sean>I should have. I should be the Stubbs guy.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.500
<v Mike>So, this is, uh, another WWF game.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.640
<v Mike>We have had one before, and that one was boringly just called WWF WrestleMania.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.440
<v Mike>Okay, that's boo, that's boring.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.520
<v Mike>Come on, we've had six of those by now.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.180
<v Mike>Uh, six pay-per-views of WrestleMania.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.720
<v Mike>Uh, they decided to change it and add challenge to the fold, because now,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.600
<v Mike>it's the debut, the video game debut of yourself.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:52.920
<v Mike>Yourself is now the custom-named wrestler in this game, where it's supposed to be you,

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:59.100
<v Mike>but it's missing the, arguably the most important feature of every wrestling game that comes out

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:06.140
<v Mike>these days, the create-a-character portion of it, where you spend hours making either yourself,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.100
<v Mike>or some clown, or some famous person, and give them a name.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.000
<v Mike>The yourself thing, as like, just seeing that in the title.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.960
<v Mike>It's a hand wave, yeah. Right. It's not you.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:23.020
<v Sean>But I really, you have to hand it to them that they knew that this is something that people wanted to do.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:29.420
<v Sean>If, if just to pretend that this is you, and not so much customize yourself, like,

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.500
<v Sean>this is just like, yeah, you can just self-insert, use your imagination.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.000
<v Mike>Right, but why can't I just self-insert my brain into the body of Hulk Hogan?

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.240
<v Sean>Well, I mean, you wouldn't want to do that now.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:50.100
<v Mike>No, yeah, yeah, well, definitely not now, but Hulk Hogan, at the time, actually was just falling

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.360
<v Mike>out of favor, if you noticed, on both the main menu, and the box, and all that.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:59.540
<v Mike>He's present on the box, of course, he's still a big draw, but the biggest draw in the WWF at this

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:04.620
<v Mike>time is the Ultimate Warrior, and WrestleMania VI had just happened earlier this year, this year,

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.600
<v Mike>1990, and he took the belt for the first time from Hulk Hogan, so it was one of those things

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:17.480
<v Mike>where now, now you have like a, you know, a new superstar, a new guy to lead the next

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:21.760
<v Mike>generation, and didn't quite pay off, and Hulk wound up winning a bunch more times, but

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.080
<v Mike>did you, did you feel like Hulk had been maybe not as big of a deal as he was in the first

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.500
<v Mike>game, now in this game, or was it still like, no, he's the biggest star?

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:39.180
<v Sean>Well, first of all, if I have, if I see Randy Savage, I guess Macho King Randy Savage

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:46.080
<v Sean>now, you have to clarify that to me, but that's my guy, but I did notice that you had to scroll

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:54.360
<v Sean>a few times to get to Hulk Hogan, so if that has anything to do with it, I could assume that

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.900
<v Sean>maybe he's fallen a bit out of favor.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.980
<v Mike>Yeah, and I wouldn't say like, not a ton, obviously, he was the main event fight against the Ultimate

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:06.820
<v Mike>Warrior, but you notice it even just in the, there's that ladder mode where you one by one

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:11.220
<v Mike>take on the opponents, and they start you with the jobbers, who aren't really jobbers

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.240
<v Mike>by any means in the game, jobbers are just people who just take hits and leave matches,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.680
<v Mike>but, you know, here it's like eight great WWF superstars, but I guess nobody's hoping

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:24.280
<v Mike>that like the big boss man's gonna become WWF world champion, right?

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.140
<v Sean>He does look the most pedestrian.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.100
<v Sean>Yeah. A cop?

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:35.020
<v Sean>Come on. Yeah, he's just sort of wearing like a, like a button-up shirt, and he's not, I don't even

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.880
<v Sean>see any like underwear looking stuff on him, it's very strange.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.360
<v Mike>Yeah, back then though, that was like, that was your personality, right?

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:47.120
<v Mike>It was like, you know, I'm a wrestler who's a cop, I'm a wrestler who's good looking, right?

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.500
<v Mike>Ravishing Rick Rude, like that's, that's not a personality, buddy.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:57.800
<v Sean>I never heard of him. I think the listeners know by now that I've never truly watched wrestling, if they've watched,

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:03.620
<v Sean>if they've listened to the rest of the episodes, but I thought they, if I didn't know better,

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:05.060
<v Sean>I would have thought that that guy was made up.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:10.060
<v Mike>That's funny, because I do remember from the last time we recorded, you and Joe both being

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:15.300
<v Mike>astonished to learn that Macho Man's theme song was Pomp and Circumstance, the graduation

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:23.500
<v Mike>music. And to me, I hear that music, and even when I was graduating, it was like, damn, that's

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:24.520
<v Mike>Macho Man's theme song.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.240
<v Mike>Like, for me, that's the Macho Man, not so much graduation, but it happens again in this

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:33.340
<v Mike>game too. The theme songs in general, though, in this game, a little weird.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.200
<v Mike>They switch back and forth between the two wrestlers, and that's fine.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.140
<v Mike>Some of them are great.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.940
<v Mike>You know, they could be great if you're doing Macho Man versus Hulk, and you're just hearing

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:45.060
<v Mike>two awesome theme songs switch back and forth.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.420
<v Mike>It's a little weirder when it's like, yourself, because like, you don't even know that theme

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.820
<v Mike>song. Like, it means nothing to you.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:57.280
<v Sean>It's a, you know, it's just a stand-in theme song.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.960
<v Sean>You can hum whatever you like when you're playing as yourself.

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:05.660
<v Mike>When you would make a character, though, in like, the THQ games, you would have to pick

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:11.180
<v Mike>a theme song. And they had a couple of, like, probably music library tracks that they were able to license

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:15.920
<v Mike>or whatever. But you would also be able to, like, just grab, like, Stone Cold Steve Austin's theme

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.820
<v Mike>song. And it's like, yeah, of course that's going to be my theme song.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:22.120
<v Mike>Why wouldn't it? But then, is that really any different than just saying, like, yourself?

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:26.680
<v Mike>Like, you do kind of, if you're going to go in with the yourself thing on a custom character,

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:31.360
<v Mike>and I ask you because I know you love the create a character things in these wrestling

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.760
<v Mike>games. Like, what is your goal when you're creating a character?

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:51.660
<v Sean>I'm trying to create a cohesive, holistic vibe of a dude or gal.

00:07:52.300 --> 00:08:00.300
<v Sean>And sometimes that means that, like, but I also have trouble using the other theme songs,

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:08.160
<v Sean>or especially in, like, the much more recent ones where you can sort of queue up and key

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:17.580
<v Sean>and DMX lightboard the entire walk-up, which I never get that into, where you have to, like,

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.700
<v Sean>pick the video that plays behind you as you're walking and stuff.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:26.620
<v Sean>Like, it's really hard when they're like, oh, yeah, do you want, I don't know, who's big

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:32.740
<v Sean>right now? Like, you have to use these, like, pre-made, like, videos that actual wrestlers use.

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:37.860
<v Sean>And it's sort of that same thing of just, like, I want something unique, and that means

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.040
<v Sean>I have to use royalty-free music.

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:46.840
<v Sean>And sometimes that means you have to use just standard, like, lightning bolt video stuff

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:52.500
<v Sean>behind you. It's, uh, I don't know what I'm saying right now, but it's the plight of the creative character

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.600
<v Sean>enjoyer. Right.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:58.760
<v Mike>And in a way, you actually are role-playing as the promoter and not necessarily the individual

00:08:58.760 --> 00:08:59.880
<v Mike>wrestler that you're making.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:05.400
<v Mike>Like, a lot of people just use the creative wrestler thing to make themselves or, uh...

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.560
<v Sean>But, like, what, are you going to give yourself, like, a t-shirt?

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.720
<v Sean>Like, are you just going to wear regular clothes?

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:13.260
<v Sean>Like, no, you've got to make yourself, but, like, cool.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.160
<v Mike>Oh, absolutely. Yeah.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:16.340
<v Mike>I'm really good at that.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:24.880
<v Mike>And I shouldn't be, like, I shouldn't be beating up the yourself character.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:31.860
<v Mike>I do think this is a, this is the earliest introduction of a you in a video game, even

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.360
<v Mike>if it isn't just, like, wrestling, right?

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:39.840
<v Mike>Like, just the idea that you're in the game, even if it is super loose here, this is, like,

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:40.880
<v Mike>the first time we're seeing that.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.220
<v Mike>And so this is a groundbreaking idea.

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:51.240
<v Sean>Absolutely. Now, before we move on, I just need you to explain to me what this macho king thing is,

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.180
<v Mike>because... Right, right.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:53.900
<v Sean>I just don't see him as that.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.520
<v Mike>It was just an arc for him in the show, and he kind of, you know...

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:04.000
<v Mike>I would never say Macho Man went full heel or full babyface at any time in his career.

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:05.320
<v Mike>He just kind of was, like...

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:08.400
<v Mike>That's a babyface. Babyface means, like, you're the crowd pleaser.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:10.560
<v Mike>Like, that's... I don't know why they came up with that term.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.200
<v Mike>I never understood it, but I'm sure there's some deep...

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.480
<v Mike>deep lore explanation for it.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.840
<v Mike>Okay. You're either a heel or you're a babyface.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:21.280
<v Mike>And I don't think he ever did too much of anything, because he was just kind of doing

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:27.640
<v Mike>his own thing. So even when he was the macho king, which probably was more heel territory, the crowd still loves

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:31.380
<v Mike>him. You know? It's like, you can't get enough of that shit either, and you love to...

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.180
<v Mike>You're not even booing him on, except for when he's, like, being rude to Miss Elizabeth,

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:36.420
<v Sean>you know? That is...

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.100
<v Sean>Yeah. He basically crowned himself macho king.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.520
<v Mike>You know? It's one of those things.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:41.640
<v Sean>Okay. Good to know.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:43.800
<v Mike>He didn't, like, win an award or something.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:44.960
<v Sean>No, I didn't think so.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:47.320
<v Sean>I did figure it was some storyline.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:50.500
<v Sean>And I know that he has many different iterations.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.020
<v Sean>I just never knew that his name would have changed.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.100
<v Mike>Yeah. Yeah, but if you think about it, right?

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.360
<v Mike>It's just... He goes from macho man to macho king.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.960
<v Mike>Just a matter of time before we met macho god.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:08.340
<v Mike>Right. Yeah. So we can get into the game, but I did just want to ask a general question, not even just

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:12.200
<v Mike>to you, but I guess to the creators of wrestling games.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:14.260
<v Mike>Now, wrestling isn't...

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:21.100
<v Mike>While it is a sport and is definitely, you know, now sports entertainment, it doesn't need

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:27.620
<v Mike>to be a sport like how baseball, football, basketball, hockey have been on the NES where,

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:31.260
<v Mike>like, they try to do, you know, there's two camps, right?

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:35.340
<v Mike>There's the simulator aspect where it's like, we want to bring baseball to the NES so that

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:36.660
<v Mike>you don't have to play it in your backyard.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:40.480
<v Mike>And then there's the, like, what if robots played baseball, right?

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.040
<v Sean>And let's do that.

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.980
<v Mike>And yet, wrestling never went there, right?

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:46.840
<v Sean>Like, why... This is...

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:48.720
<v Sean>WWF, huge brand.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:55.480
<v Mike>Uh-huh. Why would they focus on, like, just the athleticism of the wrestling and not, like, the video game

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:01.160
<v Mike>elements of, like, well, yeah, we should have power-ups and we should have, like, you know,

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.640
<v Mike>a side-scrolling game, like, right?

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:07.260
<v Mike>Where you play as, like, Hulk and take down, you know, goons and thugs.

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.680
<v Mike>It's like, why wasn't that the idea?

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:15.360
<v Mike>And instead, we get, like, these simulate wrestling when, like, the technology's not quite

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:17.260
<v Mike>there yet. I think I have an answer.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:26.740
<v Sean>Yeah, me too. And you might not like it, but I think maybe, uh, wrestling, the WWF at this time, the WWE

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:33.840
<v Sean>now, uh, they might be a little, you know, self-conscious about their image and the idea

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:38.020
<v Sean>of, uh, things not being as they seem.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:45.500
<v Sean>And, you know, if you're going to use power-ups and cartoonish, uh, like, mechanics like that,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:49.400
<v Sean>that might play into those rumors that, um...

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:50.340
<v Sean>Oh, yes.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.540
<v Sean>What you see may not be what, uh, what is real.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:58.900
<v Mike>Which they were, like, going through, like, at this very moment in 1990.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:03.700
<v Mike>Like, those cases were just starting to begin, really, and, or, or might have actually been

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:04.700
<v Mike>finalized at that point.

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:08.140
<v Mike>I'm not quite sure on the timeline of that, but it was a losing battle.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:12.000
<v Sean>Um, I think this is around the time...

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:16.300
<v Sean>So I can see, like, if there was a time where, like, they, if everybody thought, like, football

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:24.080
<v Sean>was rigged, which it, it may or may not be, um, that there wouldn't be any semblance of,

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:25.360
<v Sean>like, an NFL blitz ever.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:26.980
<v Mike>So, maybe.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:31.940
<v Sean>I don't know. This is, this is my pulled-out-of-my-ass concept.

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:37.920
<v Mike>I get it. Yeah, no, I was trying to figure out when Hulk Hogan, uh, choked out that reporter,

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.860
<v Sean>uh, on air. Um, do you know what I'm talking about?

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.300
<v Mike>Mm-mm. Okay.

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:48.720
<v Mike>Uh, he was trying to show, like, that wrestling was real or whatever, and, um, it was Richard,

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:49.540
<v Sean>uh, Belzer.

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.260
<v Sean>So he, so he assaulted a guy.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:57.000
<v Mike>Yeah, he put him in, he put him in a headlock, and unfortunately, he, you know, he didn't get

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.600
<v Mike>any air, so he passed out on stage, hit his head on the floor, too.

